8.1 Settings
============
The supported settings in a nanorc file are:

‘set afterends’			Ctrl+Right/Ctrl+Delete stop at word endings.
‘set allow_insecure_backup’	Force backup success.
				(do not enable)
				(no permission reset due to OS considerations)

‘set atblanks’			Word wrapping occurs on whitespace.
				(rather than just edge of the screen)
‘set autoindent’		Automatically indent newly created lines.

[LOG]
‘set backup’			Backup previous version of file.
				(enabled with ‘set backup’ or --backup or -B)
‘set backupdir "DIRECTORY"’	Specify backup directory.

[UI]
‘set emptyline’			Do not use the line below the title bar
‘set constantshow’		cursor position on the status bar.  
				(Note that this overrides ‘quickblank’)
‘set guidestripe NUMBER’	Draw a column ruler.
				(stripe color can be changed with ‘set stripecolor’)
‘set linenumbers’		Display line numbers.
‘set minibar’			Replace the status bar with current buffers info.
				The minibars left shows the name of the file.
					(an asterisk is shown for a modified buffer)
				The minibars right is about the cursor including:
					'current line', 'column number', 'character code'
					'file percentage', 'file status','file buffer',
					'number of lines in buffer'
					(‘set constantshow’ required for state flags)
					(‘set stateflags’ required for state flags)
					(the same flags as are shown by ‘set stateflags’)
				For multiole buffers, lines in buffer is replaced
				by the counter [i/n].
					
				line/column numbers and character-code use 
				and can be toggled on and off with ‘M-C’.
‘set keycolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				shortcut key color combination 
				(help lines at the bottom of the screen)
				(foreground and background colors are:)
				(‘red’, ‘green’, ‘blue’, ‘magenta’,)
				(‘yellow’, ‘cyan’, ‘white’, ‘black’)
				Each of these may be prefixed with ‘light’ 

				terminal emulators with 256 colors support:
				‘pink’, ‘purple’, ‘mauve’, ‘lagoon’, ‘mint’,
				‘lime’, ‘peach’, ‘orange’, ‘latte’, ‘rosy’,
				‘beet’, ‘plum’, ‘sea’, ‘sky’, ‘slate’, ‘teal’,
				‘sage’, ‘brown’, ‘ocher’, ‘sand’, ‘tawny’,
				‘brick’, ‘crimson’, and ‘normal’ 
				(‘normal’ means default foreground/background)
				3-digit hexadecimal colors are also supported.

				FGCOLOR & BGCOLOR may be preceded by bold/italic 
				(separated by commas) or be ignored completely.
‘set scrollercolor FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				indicator alias "scrollbar" color combination.
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set selectedcolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				Use this color combination for selected text.
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set spotlightcolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				search match highlighting color combination.
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set statuscolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				status bar color combination.
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set stripecolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				vertical guiding stripe color combination.  
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set titlecolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				title bar color combination.
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set promptcolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				prompt bar color combination.  
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names.)
‘set errorcolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				Status bar error message color combination.
				(the default is bold, white, red)
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set minicolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				Use this color combination for the mini bar.  
				(*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names)
‘set functioncolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
				concise function descriptions 
				(the two help lines at the bottom of the screen)
				*Note set keycolor:: for valid color names
‘set boldtext’			Use bold text rather than inverse colors
				(title bar, status bar, prompt bar, mini bar)
				(key combos, line numbers, and selected text)
				(Overridden by setting the options titlecolor)
				(statuscolor, promptcolor, minicolor, keycolor)
				(numbercolor, and/or selectedcolor)

[On Justification]
‘set bookstyle’			paragraph beginnings are lines beginning with whitespace.
					(unless auto-indenting is on)
‘set brackets "CHARACTERS"’	Enclose justified text in CHARACTERS.
					(Only closing punctuation (see ‘set punct’)
					(The default value is ""')>]}".)
‘set fill NUMBER’		set target screen wrap size to COL_NUM
					(for justification/hardwrapping)
					(If NUMBER <= 0 then screen wrap width) 
					(then screen width minus column NUMBER)
					allowing wrap point to vary on resize)
					(The default value is -8)
‘set matchbrackets CHARACTERS’	Specify the open/close brackets found 
					that can be found by bracket searches.
					(opening set must be before the closing set)
					(the two sets must be in the same order)
					(The default value is "(<[{)>]}")


[Formatting]
‘set breaklonglines’		Automatically hard-wrap the current line 
				(when it becomes overlong.)

[Functionallity]
‘set saveonexit’		Save a changed buffer automatically on exit (^X); 
‘set positionlog’		Save cursor/anchor positions between sessions.
				(remembered for the past 200 files)
‘set preserve’			Preserve the XOFF and XON sequences.
				(stop(^S)/resume(^Q) output)
‘set historylog’		log search/replacement strings and executed commands.
				(previous 100 can be reused in later sessions)
‘set cutfromcursor’		Use cut-from-cursor-to-end-of-line.
				(default is cutting the whole line)
‘set casesensitive’		Do case-sensitive searches by default.
‘set magic’			use libmagic to determine filetype.
‘set jumpyscrolling’		Scroll half-screen instead of per line.
‘set mouse’			mouse clicks can be used to place the cursor.
				(double click to set mark or execute shortcuts)
‘set multibuffer’		When reading (^R), insert file into new buffer.
‘set noconvert’			Don’t convert files from DOS/Mac format.
‘set nohelp’			Don’t display the help lists at the bottom of the screen.
‘set regexp’			Do regular-expression searches by default.  
				(RE in ‘nano’ are the extended type (ERE))

[untitled]
‘set colonparsing’		
     When a filename given on the command line ends in a colon plus
     digits and this filename does not exist, then snip the colon plus
     digits and understand the digits as a line number.  If the trimmed
     filename does not exist either, then repeat the process and
     understand the obtained two numbers as line and column number.  But
     if the doubly trimmed filename does not exist either, then forget
     the trimming and accept the original filename as is.  To disable
     this colon parsing for some file, use ‘+1’ or similar before the
     relevant filename.



‘set indicator’
     Display a "scrollbar" on the righthand side of the edit window.  It
     shows the position of the viewport in the buffer and how much of
     the buffer is covered by the viewport.

‘set locking’
     Enable vim-style lock-files for when editing files.







‘set nonewlines’
     Don’t automatically add a newline when a text does not end with
     one.  (This can cause you to save non-POSIX text files.)

‘set nowrap’
     Deprecated option since it has become the default setting.  When
     needed, use ‘unset breaklonglines’ instead.

‘set numbercolor [bold,][italic,]FGCOLOR,BGCOLOR’
     Use this color combination for line numbers.  *Note set keycolor::
     for valid color names.

‘set operatingdir "DIRECTORY"’
     At startup, make ‘nano’ change to the given DIRECTORY, and allow
     reading and writing files only in this directory and its
     subdirectories.




‘set punct "CHARACTERS"’
     Set the characters treated as closing punctuation when justifying
     paragraphs.  This may not include blank characters.  Only the
     specified closing punctuation, optionally followed by closing
     brackets (see ‘set brackets’), can end sentences.  The default
     value is "!.?".

‘set quickblank’
     Make status-bar messages disappear after 1 keystroke instead of
     after 20.  Note that option ‘constantshow’ overrides this.  When
     option ‘minibar’ or ‘zero’ is in effect, ‘quickblank’ makes a
     message disappear after 0.8 seconds instead of after the default
     1.5 seconds.

‘set quotestr "REGEX"’
     Set the regular expression for matching the quoting part of a line.
     The default value is "^([ \t]*([!#%:;>|}]|//))+".  (Note that ‘\t’
     stands for a literal Tab character.)  This makes it possible to
     rejustify blocks of quoted text when composing email, and to rewrap
     blocks of line comments when writing source code.

‘set rawsequences’
     Interpret escape sequences directly, instead of asking ‘ncurses’ to
     translate them.  (If you need this option to get some keys to work
     properly, it means that the terminfo terminal description that is
     used does not fully match the actual behavior of your terminal.
     This can happen when you ssh into a BSD machine, for example.)
     Using this option disables ‘nano’’s mouse support.

‘set rebinddelete’
     Interpret the ‘Delete’ and ‘Backspace’ keys differently so that
     both work properly.  You should only use this option when on your
     system either ‘Backspace’ acts like Delete or ‘Delete’ acts like
     Backspace.



‘set showcursor’
     Put the cursor on the highlighted item in the file browser, and
     show the cursor in the help viewer, to aid braille users and people
     with poor vision.

‘set smarthome’
     Make the Home key smarter.  When Home is pressed anywhere but at
     the very beginning of non-whitespace characters on a line, the
     cursor jumps to that beginning (either forwards or backwards).  If
     the cursor is already at that position, it jumps to the true
     beginning of the line.

‘set softwrap’
     Display lines that exceed the screen’s width over multiple screen
     lines.  (You can make this soft-wrapping occur at whitespace
     instead of rudely at the screen’s edge, by using also ‘set
     atblanks’.)

‘set speller "PROGRAM [ARGUMENT ...]"’
     Use the given program to do spell checking and correcting.  *Note
     --speller:: for details.



‘set stateflags’
     Use the top-right corner of the screen for showing some state
     flags: ‘I’ when auto-indenting, ‘M’ when the mark is on, ‘L’ when
     hard-wrapping (breaking long lines), ‘R’ when recording a macro,
     and ‘S’ when soft-wrapping.  When the buffer is modified, a star
     (‘*’) is shown after the filename in the center of the title bar.







‘set tabsize NUMBER’
     Use a tab size of NUMBER columns.  The value of NUMBER must be
     greater than 0.  The default value is 8.

‘set tabstospaces’
     Convert each typed tab to spaces — to the number of spaces that a
     tab at that position would take up.  (Note: pasted tabs are not
     converted.)


‘set trimblanks’
     Remove trailing whitespace from wrapped lines when automatic
     hard-wrapping occurs or when text is justified.

‘set unix’
     Save a file by default in Unix format.  This overrides nano’s
     default behavior of saving a file in the format that it had.  (This
     option has no effect when you also use ‘set noconvert’.)

‘set whitespace "CHARACTERS"’
     Set the two characters used to indicate the presence of tabs and
     spaces.  They must be single-column characters.  The default pair
     for a UTF-8 locale is "»·", and for other locales ">.".

‘set wordbounds’
     Detect word boundaries differently by treating punctuation
     characters as part of a word.

‘set wordchars "CHARACTERS"’
     Specify which other characters (besides the normal alphanumeric
     ones) should be considered as parts of words.  When using this
     option, you probably want to unset ‘wordbounds’.

‘set zap’
     Let an unmodified ‘Backspace’ or ‘Delete’ erase the marked region
     (instead of a single character, and without affecting the
     cutbuffer).

‘set zero’			Hide all interface elements and use all 
				terminal rows for displaying buffer contents.
				(title bar, status bar, and help lines) 
				(status bar appears with significant messages)
				(‘M-Z’ toggles the title/status bar)
				(‘M-X’ toggles the help lines.)
